<div class="container">
  <header class="lesson-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Tool</p>
    <h1>The <em>Metronome</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">Every page on Drum Town carries a metronome — the tempo pill in the header opens it. Tap the pill and the controls appear across the bottom of the screen. Set a tempo, pick a time signature, and press play. This page is about using it well.</p>
    {#- §8.5. An enhancement over the pill, never a replacement for it: the
        button ships hidden and only the script below reveals it, so a page
        without JS shows exactly the one control that works — the pill. -#}
    <button class="btn btn--primary" id="metronome-open" type="button" aria-controls="site-metronome" hidden>Open the metronome <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></button>
  </header>

  <article class="lesson-prose">
    <h2 class="section-label">Quick Start</h2>
    <p>Tap the tempo pill in the header to open the metronome along the bottom of the screen. Type a tempo (or drag the slider), choose beats per bar — 2/4 through 7/8 — and press play. The first beat of each bar is accented so you always know where "1" is.</p>

    <h2 class="section-label">What Tempo Should I Practice At?</h2>
    <ul class="bulleted">
      <li><strong>Learning something new:</strong> slower than feels necessary — ♩ = 50–70. If it feels boring, it's right. (See <a href="/lessons/slow-practice/">Slow Practice</a>.)</li>
      <li><strong>Cleaning something up:</strong> 10 BPM below the tempo where it falls apart, then move up in small steps.</li>
      <li><strong>Building speed:</strong> raise the tempo ~10% only after a passage is 100% clean for two unbroken minutes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="section-label">Three Metronome Games</h2>
    <p>A click doesn't have to mark every beat. Three ways to make it harder and more useful, from <a href="/lessons/metronome-practice/">Practicing with a Metronome</a>:</p>
    <ul class="bulleted">
      <li><strong>Click on 2 and 4</strong> — halve the tempo and hear the click as the backbeat. The click becomes a bandmate instead of a ruler.</li>
      <li><strong>Click once per bar</strong> — you hold the time for three beats alone and the click grades you on beat 1.</li>
      <li><strong>Subdivision check</strong> — play 8ths or 16ths against a quarter click and listen for the notes between clicks drifting.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The metronome follows you to every lesson on the site. The sound does not: a browser only lets audio start from a tap on the page you are looking at, so opening a new lesson pauses the click. The dock comes back open with your tempo and meter still set and the play button waiting — one press and it is running again.</p>
  </article>
</div>

<script>
  (function () {
    // Everything is looked up on DOMContentLoaded, not at parse time. This
    // script sits inside <main>, and the dock is an <aside> that the layout
    // prints AFTER </main> — at parse time getElementById('site-metronome')
    // is null and an eager version of this bailed out silently. Waiting also
    // means the deferred metronome.js has already bound the pill before the
    // button is revealed, so it is never visible in a state where pressing it
    // would do nothing.
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
      var opener = document.getElementById('metronome-open');
      var pill   = document.getElementById('metronome-pill');
      var dock   = document.getElementById('site-metronome');
      if (!opener || !pill || !dock) return;

      // dc_metro_collapsed persists, so the second time anyone visits this page
      // the dock is ALREADY open on first paint — and a button still reading
      // "Open the metronome" would then be a lie that silently no-ops. Drive the
      // label from the dock's real state. The observer matters because the dock
      // can also be toggled from the header pill, the collapse chevron or Escape
      // while the reader is sitting on this page.
      function syncOpener() {
        var open = !dock.classList.contains('is-collapsed');
        opener.firstChild.nodeValue = open ? 'Jump to the metronome ' : 'Open the metronome ';
      }
      syncOpener();
      new MutationObserver(syncOpener).observe(dock, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['class'] });
      opener.hidden = false;

      opener.addEventListener('click', function () {
        // The pill is a real toggle. Pressing it while the dock is already open
        // would CLOSE it, and a button reading "Open the metronome" must never
        // do that; when the dock is already open the useful move is to hand
        // focus to the play button, which is where the pill's open path lands.
        if (dock.classList.contains('is-collapsed')) {
          pill.click();
          return;
        }
        var go = document.getElementById('metronome-toggle');
        if (go) go.focus();
      });
    }, { once: true });
  })();
</script>
